<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/7d5d4cbb6d87> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "Jaywalking AI Mistakenly Shames Woman in Advertisement" ;
    airo:sourceDocId "514" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/89bed1fbf390> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "CNET also reported recently that Chinese authorities have been using gait recognition for public surveillance in Beijing and Shanghai." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "513" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/8b7af464de9e> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "Since last year, many cities across China have cracked down on jaywalking by investing in facial recognition systems and advanced AI-powered surveillance cameras." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "519" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/8e6dd1ee734a> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "Dong Mingzhu, who was in the ad and is head of China's largest air conditioning manufacturer Gree Electric, had her face on display in the city as punishment for jaywalking." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "514" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/99f5c80c63a2> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "Chinese facial recognition system mistakes a face on a bus for a jaywalker" ;
    airo:sourceDocId "5070" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/9a9da9a32562> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "Chinese facial recognition system catches jaywalker, turns out to be a bus" ;
    airo:sourceDocId "512" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/a077d77c374b> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "That artificial intelligence-backed surveillance system, however, erred in capturing Dong’s image on Wednesday from an advertisement on the side of a moving bus." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "519" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/a6030d952b43> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "[500] At 1 intersection in the eastern city of Ningbo, the face of famous Chinese businesswoman Dong Mingzhu was displayed on a public screen dedicated to “naming and shaming” jaywalkers caught by the city’s facial recognition system." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "500" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/a7b14b6c64df> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "China has been flirting with the idea of automatically fining jaywalkers caught on camera, and with 14,000 people named and shamed at one crossroad in Shenzhen in a ten-month period, this really highlights just how badly that could backfire." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "505" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/ae6ec3b70b47> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "[511] A facial recognition system in the city of Ningbo caught Dong Mingzhu, the chair of appliance-making giant Gree Electric, running a red light." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "511" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/b807326074d0> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "AI snaps business titan jaywalking" ;
    airo:sourceDocId "499" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/b8ab5b8273ee> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "Chinese businesswoman accused of jaywalking after AI camera spots her face on an advert" ;
    airo:sourceDocId "507" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/b8e187f38e65> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "Chinese police have admitted to wrongly shaming a famous businesswoman after a facial recognition system designed to catch jaywalkers mistook an advert on the side of a bus for her actual face." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "507" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/b9295fe4856f> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "While China has moved ahead of the rest of the world in making facial recognition technology a part of people’s everyday life, there are systems in the country that need tweaking." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "508" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/bd5412cde9c2> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "Facial recognition system accuses woman in bus ad of jaywalking" ;
    airo:sourceDocId "505" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/c1b7b24d8023> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "[5070] Local police soon admitted in a statement on microblogging site Weibo that identifying Dong as a jaywalker was an error made by the facial recognition system, and claimed that the problem had now been fixed by an upgrade." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "5070" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/c3e1d28d37aa> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "Chinese facial recognition flags bus ad woman for jaywalking" ;
    airo:sourceDocId "506" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/c4bfc400d034> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "[511] A facial recognition system in the city of Ningbo caught Dong Mingzhu, the chair of appliance-making giant Gree Electric, running a red light." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "511" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/cbc93cfcf016> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "Several companies, such as Dahua, have been working to upgrade the performance of their smart city systems, as China seeks to leverage urban security networks that the New York Times has reported include 200 million surveillance cameras." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "513" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/ce3d89f2f176> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "Making a compelling case for change is the recent experience of Dong Mingzhu, chairwoman of China’s biggest maker of air conditioners Gree Electric Appliances, who found her face splashed on a huge screen erected along a street in the port city of Ningbo that displays images of people caught jaywalking by surveillance cameras." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "519" .

